dates by simply uploading a Markdown file.\\\\n\\\\n1.  **S3 Bucket Strategy:**\\\\n    *   **Uploads Bucket (`[project]-uploads`):** A private bucket where the user uploads raw `.md` content files. Versioning and server-side encryption will be enabled.\\\\n    *   **Content Bucket (`[project]-content`):** A public-facing bucket configured for static website hosting. It will store the processed HTML files and a master `posts.json` index file for dynamically listing posts.\\\\n\\\\n2.  **Automated Processing Workflow:**\\\\n    *   An **S3 Event Notification** will be configured on the uploads bucket. It will trigger exclusively on the creation of objects with a `.md` suffix.\\\\n    *   This event will invoke a `ContentProcessor` Lambda function.\\\\n\\\\n3.  **Lambda Function (`ContentProcessor`):**\\\\n    *   **Trigger:** Receives the S3 event, identifying the uploaded file.\\\\n    *   **Action:** Downloads the Markdown file, converts it to an HTML fragment using a standard library, and uploads the resulting `.html` file to a `posts/` directory in the public content bucket.\\\\n    *   **Indexing:** After a successful conversion, the Lambda will update a `posts.json` file in the root of the content bucket. This JSON file will contain metadata for all posts (title, path, date), allowing the frontend to easily generate a list of all news updates.\\\\n\\\\n4.  **IAM Role (`ContentProcessorLambdaRole`):**\\\\n    *   A dedicated IAM role will be created for the Lambda function.\\\\n    *   **Permissions:** The attached policy will grant the minimum necessary permissions: `s3:GetObject` on the uploads bucket, `s3:GetObject`/`s3:PutObject` on the content bucket, and standard permissions for writing logs to CloudWatch.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n### Part 2: Contact Form Backend Architecture\\\\n\\\\nThis system provides a secure and reliable backend for the website's contact form.\\\\n\\\\n1.  **API Endpoint (API Gateway):**\\\\n    *   A REST API will be created with a `/contact` resource that accepts `POST` requests.\\\\n    *   The endpoint will be configured with **Lambda Proxy Integration**, immediately forwarding requests to our backend function.\\\\n    *   **CORS** will be enabled to allow the frontend website to make requests to the API.\\\\n\\\\n2.  **Lambda Function (`ContactFormHandler`):**\\\\n    *   **Trigger:** Invoked by the API Gateway.\\\\n    *   **Action:** Parses the incoming JSON body (`name`, `email`, `message`), performs basic validation, and uses the AWS SDK to send an email via SES.\\\\n    *   **User Experience:** The `Reply-To` header of the email will be set to the user's provided email address, allowing the HOA to reply directly to the sender.\\\\n    *   **Configuration:** Key details like the 'To' and 'From' email addresses will be managed securely via Lambda environment variables.\\\\n\\\\n3.  **Email Service (AWS SES):**\\\\n    *   **Identity Verification:** An HOA email address (e.g., `contact@your-hoa.com`) will be verified in SES to act as the recipient and a `no-reply@your-hoa.com` address will be verified as the sender.\\\\n    *   **Production Access:** A request will be filed with AWS to move the account out of the SES sandbox to enable sending emails to any address.\\\\n\\\\n4.  **IAM & Permissions:**\\\\n    *   **Lambda Role (`ContactFormLambdaRole`):** A role will be created with permissions to write logs and a specific inline policy allowing the `ses:SendEmail` action.\\\\n    *   **API Gateway Invocation:** A resource-based policy will be attached to the Lambda function, granting the API Gateway service principal the explicit permission to invoke it.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\nThis combined plan provides a robust, scalable, and secure foundation for the website's dynamic content and communication needs. All required IAM permissions and service configurations have been specified.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"content-comm-plan-01\\\"\\n}\\n###report-content-comm-plan-01###\"}]